<article class="sp-view">
<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">23 January 1872 · San Francisco, California</p><h1 class="sp-title">Itō Hirobumi's Rising Sun Speech</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>The Iwakura Mission, Japanese leaders, officials and students sent by the new Meiji government to study the West and to open talks on revising the unequal treaties, reached San Francisco on 15 January 1872. At a banquet the city's leading citizens gave the embassy, Itō Hirobumi, one of its four deputy ambassadors, answered the toast to the guests, reading his address in English.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>The red disk in the centre of our national flag shall no longer appear like a wafer over a sealed empire, but henceforth be in fact what it is designed to be, the noble emblem of the rising sun, moving onward and upward amid the enlightened nations of the world.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">From The Japanese in America (1872), edited by Charles Lanman, which prints the address as Itō read it in English.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“The red disk in the centre of our national flag shall no longer appear like a wafer over a sealed empire, but henceforth be in fact what it is designed to be, the noble emblem of the rising sun, moving onward and upward amid the enlightened nations of the world.” — Itō Hirobumi, Itō Hirobumi's Rising Sun Speech" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-ito-rising-sun.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The address was widely reported and became known as the &quot;rising sun speech&quot; (hinomaru enzetsu). The mission went on by rail to Washington, D.C., but failed to renegotiate the unequal treaties and sailed for Britain in August 1872. Itō became Japan's first prime minister in 1885 and oversaw the drafting of the Meiji Constitution of 1889.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a></div></div></section>
